[
  {
    "name": "resource_status",
    "description": "Read registered shared runners, queue tickets, duplicate markers and mirror freshness. An unknown or stale mirror is not a successful result.",
    "input_schema": {
      "type": "object",
      "properties": {
        "resource": {
          "type": "string"
        }
      },
      "required": []
    },
    "defer_loading": true
  },
  {
    "name": "ask_user",
    "description": "Ask the user one or more multiple-choice questions on their iPhone (a card in this conversation plus a push notification) and wait for the answers. Use it to confirm anything risky (sending messages / email for them, deleting, changing live systems) or to let them pick between options. AskUserQuestion does not work here; use this. They can also type a free-form answer. Waits up to 2 hours; if the user writes in this conversation instead of picking an option, the question is withdrawn and the tool returns at once, with their message following. If nobody answers in time, the question becomes a to-do that waits for the user's call (in their to-do list and the next briefing), and their answer comes to this conversation later as a new message. With a deadline (each question then recommends one of its options): if nobody answers by it and you marked it reversible, the recommended options are taken by default and recorded on the question's to-do; one that cannot be undone is only reminded again and keeps waiting. With wait false the card goes up the same way but the call returns at once: end the turn without guessing the answer; it comes later as a new message quoting the question and the answer (or saying the card was closed unanswered, or expired and became a to-do). Use wait false only where your instructions say to; a confirmation this same turn needs (approving a message before it is sent) keeps the default. A waiting call holds this turn, so anything else sent to this conversation waits behind it: once such a message waits and the question has been up for a couple of minutes, the call returns without the answer and the card stays up as with wait false; end the turn then.\n\nWrite newly generated user-facing arguments, such as titles, reasons, criteria and messages, in English unless the user requests another language or the field requires source wording. Preserve quoted source text, identifiers, tool and parameter names, enum values and command prefixes exactly. Language does not change approval requirements or permission boundaries.",
    "input_schema": {
      "type": "object",
      "required": [
        "questions"
      ],
      "properties": {
        "wait": {
          "type": "boolean",
          "description": "default true: hold this turn until the user answers. false: put the card up and return at once; the answer arrives later as a new message in this conversation"
        },
        "deadline": {
          "type": "string",
          "description": "optional: until when the questions wait, a duration (\"30m\", \"4h\") or an RFC 3339 time, 1 minute to 30 days ahead; needs reversible and a recommend on every question"
        },
        "reversible": {
          "type": "boolean",
          "description": "with deadline: whether going ahead with the recommended options can be undone later. false for deleting, sending anything to other people, spending money or changing production: those are never taken by default"
        },
        "questions": {
          "type": "array",
          "minItems": 1,
          "maxItems": 4,
          "items": {
            "type": "object",
            "required": [
              "question",
              "options"
            ],
            "properties": {
              "question": {
                "type": "string"
              },
              "header": {
                "type": "string",
                "description": "short label, ≤12 chars"
              },
              "multiSelect": {
                "type": "boolean"
              },
              "recommend": {
                "type": "string",
                "description": "the label of the option you recommend; required with deadline"
              },
              "options": {
                "type": "array",
                "minItems": 2,
                "maxItems": 4,
                "items": {
                  "type": "object",
                  "required": [
                    "label"
                  ],
                  "properties": {
                    "label": {
                      "type": "string"
                    },
                    "description": {
                      "type": "string"
                    }
                  }
                }
              }
            }
          }
        }
      }
    }
  },
  {
    "name": "notify_user",
    "description": "Send the user a push notification right now (works when the app is closed). Tapping it opens this conversation. Use for results of scheduled runs and anything time-sensitive; ordinary replies already notify. In the hub's push grading, a notice from a conversation other than the main one is held for the briefing instead of alerting unless it is P0; the tool answer then says it was moved to the briefing, so treat the user as not alerted.\n\nWrite newly generated user-facing arguments, such as titles, reasons, criteria and messages, in English unless the user requests another language or the field requires source wording. Preserve quoted source text, identifiers, tool and parameter names, enum values and command prefixes exactly. Language does not change approval requirements or permission boundaries.",
    "input_schema": {
      "type": "object",
      "required": [
        "title",
        "body"
      ],
      "properties": {
        "title": {
          "type": "string"
        },
        "body": {
          "type": "string"
        },
        "push_level": {
          "type": "string",
          "enum": [
            "P0",
            "P1"
          ],
          "description": "How urgent this is. P1 (default): worth knowing; in the hub's push grading it is held for the briefing instead of alerting, and from a non-main conversation it is held even without an explicit level. P0: something the user must do or decide themselves (an action only they can take, a decision only they can make), so it alerts even in push grading and from any conversation. Use P1 for results, progress and conclusions."
        },
        "related_key": {
          "type": "string",
          "description": "Optional: what this is about, such as a PR link or a to-do id. In push grading the same key interrupts at most once in 12 hours, and held notices about it are listed together."
        }
      }
    }
  },
  {
    "name": "supervision_finish",
    "description": "Finish handling a supervision wake-up without cluttering the user's main conversation. Use visibility=\"silent\" when no user decision or useful result needs to be shown, with a short summary of what you checked or changed; then end the turn. The complete turn stays available in the expandable supervision record. Use visibility=\"user\" to explicitly show the turn without sending a notification. notify_user, ask_user, and task_review user conclusion cards also promote the turn. A promoted turn cannot be made silent again. This tool is only for supervision-triggered turns, not normal user messages.\n\nWrite newly generated user-facing arguments, such as titles, reasons, criteria and messages, in English unless the user requests another language or the field requires source wording. Preserve quoted source text, identifiers, tool and parameter names, enum values and command prefixes exactly. Language does not change approval requirements or permission boundaries.",
    "input_schema": {
      "type": "object",
      "required": [
        "visibility",
        "summary"
      ],
      "properties": {
        "visibility": {
          "type": "string",
          "enum": [
            "silent",
            "user"
          ]
        },
        "summary": {
          "type": "string",
          "minLength": 1,
          "maxLength": 1000
        }
      },
      "additionalProperties": false
    }
  },
  {
    "name": "schedule_followup",
    "description": "Wake yourself later with `prompt`; `new_conversation: true` uses its own chat. Wakes reset the message hop chain at most once per 10 minutes. `at` accepts RFC3339 or relative 30m/2h/1d. For repeats set `every` (5m-7d) once; never chain runs. Or use `cron` instead of at/every: 5 fields in the user's zone, at least 5m apart; @hourly/@daily/@weekly/@monthly/@yearly work. Day 0/7 is Sunday; restricted day fields match either. Inspect the reply's next runs. `until` cannot be a bare clock time.\n\nRuns inherit Main chat's launcher/model/effort; overrides require an own conversation, and permission mode stays the user's choice. `inbox` is only for requested Inbox results. `fresh_each_run: true` starts a new chat each run with its state note. See field descriptions for push and end-time edit rules.\n\nFor approved recurring work advancing a supervised to-do, pass its explicit `todo_id`; do not guess from a title. Omission keeps an association; \"\" clears it; one-shots refuse it. From the second run, progress is new Agent answers or tool calls (not waits or repeats), criterion changes or progress reports; a long stall asks Change approach / Keep waiting, even with `push: asked`. Get owner consent before creating or changing recurring work.\n\nWrite newly generated user-facing arguments, such as titles, reasons, criteria and messages, in English unless the user requests another language or the field requires source wording. Preserve quoted source text, identifiers, tool and parameter names, enum values and command prefixes exactly. Language does not change approval requirements or permission boundaries.",
    "input_schema": {
      "type": "object",
      "required": [
        "prompt"
      ],
      "properties": {
        "todo_id": {
          "type": "string",
          "description": "explicit supervised to-do this recurring follow-up advances"
        },
        "fresh_each_run": {
          "type": "boolean"
        },
        "at": {
          "type": "string",
          "description": "when (the first run, with every); default with every: one interval from now"
        },
        "every": {
          "type": "string",
          "description": "repeat this often, e.g. 30m"
        },
        "cron": {
          "type": "string",
          "description": "repeat on a cron expression instead of at / every: minute hour day-of-month month day-of-week in the user's time zone, e.g. */30 9-18 * * 1-5"
        },
        "prompt": {
          "type": "string",
          "description": "what to do then, self-contained"
        },
        "title": {
          "type": "string",
          "description": "short label shown in the app"
        },
        "new_conversation": {
          "type": "boolean",
          "description": "start a fresh conversation instead of continuing this one"
        },
        "push": {
          "type": "string",
          "enum": [
            "every",
            "asked"
          ],
          "description": "every (default for a new task): each run's answer is pushed to the user; asked: only what a run sends with notify_user, for a task that watches something or a standing loop (a patrol, a daily round). Continuing this conversation, a run's answer is then not pushed or sent to channels; from Main chat it runs in its own conversation instead. Omit to keep an existing task's"
        },
        "inbox": {
          "type": "string",
          "enum": [
            "off",
            "worthy",
            "every"
          ],
          "description": "what of a run goes into the user's Inbox, independent of push: off (default for a new task), worthy (a run puts a row there with inbox_post only when it finds something worth a look), every (every run ends with a row made by Persona). Set it only when the user asked for it. Omit to keep an existing task's"
        },
        "until": {
          "type": [
            "string",
            "null"
          ],
          "description": "optional end time (RFC 3339 or 30m / 2h / 1d). After it, Persona turns the task off and records a skipped run instead of running. Omit to keep an existing task's end time; null clears it. Ignored for a one-off."
        },
        "launcher": {
          "type": "string",
          "description": "this task's own launcher, instead of Main chat's (a name on the computer where its runs start). Needs new_conversation: true: a follow-up that continues this conversation runs on that conversation's launcher. Empty clears the override and follows Main chat again. Omit to keep an existing task's"
        },
        "model": {
          "type": "string",
          "description": "model id for the launcher above; without a launcher it pins the launcher the task runs on now. Empty uses the launcher's default. Omit to keep an existing task's"
        },
        "effort": {
          "type": "string",
          "enum": [
            "low",
            "medium",
            "high",
            "xhigh",
            "max"
          ],
          "description": "thinking effort for Claude launchers; without a launcher it pins the launcher the task runs on now. Omit to keep an existing task's; empty clears it"
        }
      }
    }
  },
  {
    "name": "schedule_routine",
    "description": "Create a recurring routine, or pass `id` from list_followups to edit only the fields supplied. `enabled: false` pauses it. Use `time` with optional `weekdays`, or `cron` instead. Times use the user's zone; cron has 5 fields, runs at least 5m apart, supports the listed aliases, treats 0/7 as Sunday and restricted day fields as either. Seconds, @every and TZ= are refused; inspect the reply's next runs. `until` cannot be a bare clock time.\n\nRuns inherit Main chat's launcher/model/effort. Omit overrides to keep them; an empty launcher returns to Main chat. Permission mode stays the user's choice. `inbox` is only for requested Inbox results. `fresh_each_run: true` starts a new chat per run with its state note. See field descriptions for push and end-time edit rules.\n\nFor approved work advancing a supervised to-do, pass its explicit `todo_id`; this conversation must supervise it. Omission keeps its association; \"\" clears it. From the second run Persona checks Agent answers, tool calls (not waits or repeats), criterion changes and progress reports; a long stall asks Change approach / Keep waiting, even with `push: asked`. Get owner consent before creating or changing recurring work.\n\nWrite newly generated user-facing arguments, such as titles, reasons, criteria and messages, in English unless the user requests another language or the field requires source wording. Preserve quoted source text, identifiers, tool and parameter names, enum values and command prefixes exactly. Language does not change approval requirements or permission boundaries.",
    "input_schema": {
      "type": "object",
      "properties": {
        "todo_id": {
          "type": "string",
          "description": "explicit supervised to-do this routine advances"
        },
        "fresh_each_run": {
          "type": "boolean"
        },
        "id": {
          "type": "string",
          "description": "to change an existing routine"
        },
        "title": {
          "type": "string",
          "description": "short name shown in the app, e.g. 早报"
        },
        "prompt": {
          "type": "string",
          "description": "what to do each time, self-contained"
        },
        "time": {
          "type": "string",
          "description": "HH:MM, 24-hour"
        },
        "weekdays": {
          "type": "array",
          "items": {
            "type": "integer",
            "minimum": 1,
            "maximum": 7
          },
          "description": "1 = Monday … 7 = Sunday; omit or empty = every day"
        },
        "cron": {
          "type": "string",
          "description": "instead of time / weekdays: minute hour day-of-month month day-of-week in the user's time zone, e.g. */30 9-18 * * 1-5 or 0 10 1 * *; or @hourly / @daily / @weekly / @monthly / @yearly"
        },
        "enabled": {
          "type": "boolean"
        },
        "report": {
          "type": "boolean",
          "description": "a daily report (such as a leader conversation's): each run opens with Persona's north-star lines (today's interruptions and availability, the change against yesterday until the same time, the source), which the report starts with. Omit to keep an existing routine's"
        },
        "push": {
          "type": "string",
          "enum": [
            "every",
            "asked"
          ],
          "description": "every (default for a new task): each run's answer is pushed to the user; asked: only what a run sends with notify_user, for a task that watches something (it then runs in its own conversation). Omit to keep an existing task's"
        },
        "inbox": {
          "type": "string",
          "enum": [
            "off",
            "worthy",
            "every"
          ],
          "description": "what of a run goes into the user's Inbox, independent of push: off (default for a new task), worthy (a run puts a row there with inbox_post only when it finds something worth a look; for a task that watches something), every (every run ends with a row made by Persona; for a briefing the user follows daily). Set it only when the user asked for it. Omit to keep an existing task's"
        },
        "run_if": {
          "type": [
            "object",
            "null"
          ],
          "description": "Optional owner-approved shell check on the routine computer before scheduled runs. Exit 0 runs; exit 1 skips. Other errors follow on_error. Omit to keep it or use null to remove it.",
          "properties": {
            "command": {
              "type": "string",
              "maxLength": 4096
            },
            "timeout_s": {
              "type": "integer",
              "minimum": 1,
              "maximum": 60,
              "default": 30
            },
            "on_error": {
              "type": "string",
              "enum": [
                "run",
                "skip"
              ],
              "default": "run"
            }
          },
          "required": [
            "command"
          ],
          "additionalProperties": false
        },
        "until": {
          "type": [
            "string",
            "null"
          ],
          "description": "optional end time (RFC 3339 or 30m / 2h / 1d). After it, Persona turns the task off and records a skipped run instead of running. Omit to keep an existing task's end time; null clears it. Ignored for a one-off."
        },
        "launcher": {
          "type": "string",
          "description": "this routine's own launcher, instead of Main chat's (a name on the computer where its runs start). Empty clears the override and follows Main chat again. Omit to keep an existing routine's"
        },
        "model": {
          "type": "string",
          "description": "model id for the launcher above; without a launcher it pins the launcher the routine runs on now. Empty uses the launcher's default. Omit to keep an existing routine's"
        },
        "effort": {
          "type": "string",
          "enum": [
            "low",
            "medium",
            "high",
            "xhigh",
            "max"
          ],
          "description": "thinking effort for Claude launchers; without a launcher it pins the launcher the routine runs on now. Omit to keep an existing routine's; empty clears it"
        }
      }
    }
  },
  {
    "name": "schedule_state_set",
    "description": "Stage the recurring task's state note for this scheduled run. Call near the end of the run with the run key in the scheduled prompt and the full replacement state (up to 4096 UTF-8 bytes). Keep cursors, references, and what was already reported. The hub attempts to save the draft after confirming that the run succeeded. If it cannot track the run or write the note, later runs use the last saved note. An empty string clears the note when saved.\n\nWrite newly generated user-facing arguments, such as titles, reasons, criteria and messages, in English unless the user requests another language or the field requires source wording. Preserve quoted source text, identifiers, tool and parameter names, enum values and command prefixes exactly. Language does not change approval requirements or permission boundaries.",
    "input_schema": {
      "type": "object",
      "required": [
        "run_key",
        "text"
      ],
      "properties": {
        "run_key": {
          "type": "string"
        },
        "text": {
          "type": "string"
        }
      }
    },
    "defer_loading": true
  },
  {
    "name": "delete_routine",
    "description": "Delete a scheduled task (routine or follow-up) by id (from list_followups). Confirm with the user first unless they asked for it.\n\nWrite newly generated user-facing arguments, such as titles, reasons, criteria and messages, in English unless the user requests another language or the field requires source wording. Preserve quoted source text, identifiers, tool and parameter names, enum values and command prefixes exactly. Language does not change approval requirements or permission boundaries.",
    "input_schema": {
      "type": "object",
      "required": [
        "id"
      ],
      "properties": {
        "id": {
          "type": "string"
        }
      }
    },
    "defer_loading": true
  },
  {
    "name": "list_followups",
    "description": "List every scheduled task (with ids): follow-ups that have not run yet, repeating ones, daily routines. A repeating task with an end time shows that time.\n\nWrite newly generated user-facing arguments, such as titles, reasons, criteria and messages, in English unless the user requests another language or the field requires source wording. Preserve quoted source text, identifiers, tool and parameter names, enum values and command prefixes exactly. Language does not change approval requirements or permission boundaries.",
    "input_schema": {
      "type": "object",
      "properties": {}
    }
  },
  {
    "name": "cancel_followup",
    "description": "Cancel a scheduled follow-up or a repeating task by id (from list_followups).\n\nWrite newly generated user-facing arguments, such as titles, reasons, criteria and messages, in English unless the user requests another language or the field requires source wording. Preserve quoted source text, identifiers, tool and parameter names, enum values and command prefixes exactly. Language does not change approval requirements or permission boundaries.",
    "input_schema": {
      "type": "object",
      "required": [
        "id"
      ],
      "properties": {
        "id": {
          "type": "string"
        }
      }
    }
  },
  {
    "name": "list_places",
    "description": "The user's saved places (home, the office, …) with their coordinates and reminder radius, as the user set them in Settings › Assistant › Saved Places or as you saved them with save_place. Read them before a reminder by place, and before a route or directions to or from a place the user names (home, the office); never take a place's coordinates from memory.\n\nWrite newly generated user-facing arguments, such as titles, reasons, criteria and messages, in English unless the user requests another language or the field requires source wording. Preserve quoted source text, identifiers, tool and parameter names, enum values and command prefixes exactly. Language does not change approval requirements or permission boundaries.",
    "input_schema": {
      "type": "object",
      "properties": {}
    },
    "defer_loading": true
  },
  {
    "name": "save_place",
    "description": "Save a place to the user's saved places, or change the saved place of the same name. Only for a place the user just confirmed in this conversation (their current location while they are there, or a phone_location_search result they picked); the user sees and edits it in Settings › Assistant › Saved Places.\n\nWrite newly generated user-facing arguments, such as titles, reasons, criteria and messages, in English unless the user requests another language or the field requires source wording. Preserve quoted source text, identifiers, tool and parameter names, enum values and command prefixes exactly. Language does not change approval requirements or permission boundaries.",
    "input_schema": {
      "type": "object",
      "required": [
        "name",
        "latitude",
        "longitude"
      ],
      "properties": {
        "name": {
          "type": "string",
          "description": "What the user calls it, e.g. 家 or 公司 (at most 40 characters)"
        },
        "address": {
          "type": "string",
          "description": "The address, as phone_location_search gave it"
        },
        "latitude": {
          "type": "number"
        },
        "longitude": {
          "type": "number"
        },
        "radius_meters": {
          "type": "integer",
          "description": "The reminder circle, 100–2000; leave it out for 150"
        }
      }
    },
    "defer_loading": true
  },
  {
    "name": "machines_list",
    "description": "List computers' connection, system, resources, agents, launcher failures and node builds. Samples over 30 seconds old are stale. Nulls have reasons; do not infer or rank.\n\nWrite newly generated user-facing arguments, such as titles, reasons, criteria and messages, in English unless the user requests another language or the field requires source wording. Preserve quoted source text, identifiers, tool and parameter names, enum values and command prefixes exactly. Language does not change approval requirements or permission boundaries.",
    "input_schema": {
      "type": "object",
      "properties": {}
    }
  },
  {
    "name": "machine_redeploy",
    "description": "Redeploy by ID or exact name; return the confirmed outcome.\n\nWrite newly generated user-facing arguments, such as titles, reasons, criteria and messages, in English unless the user requests another language or the field requires source wording. Preserve quoted source text, identifiers, tool and parameter names, enum values and command prefixes exactly. Language does not change approval requirements or permission boundaries.",
    "input_schema": {
      "type": "object",
      "required": [
        "machine"
      ],
      "properties": {
        "machine": {
          "type": "string"
        }
      }
    },
    "defer_loading": true
  },
  {
    "name": "agents_list",
    "description": "The Claude Code / Codex agents running on the user's computers (herdr), with their state: needs_you (waiting for an answer / approval), working, unread (finished, result not looked at), idle. done lists work finished in the last 24 hours, and muted lists the muted agents that would otherwise appear in the live snapshot; both include the work conclusion when known. Each line has machine, session id, project, title, last update and the last thing the agent said. launcher= names the launcher an agent runs on when it is known, and usage_limit (with resets_at when the reset is known) marks one its subscription's usage limit stopped (agent_switch moves such an agent to another launcher when the user asks). Known Claude Code switch states appear as ultracode=on/off; an omitted state is unknown. ultracode=on means every substantive task runs a multi-agent dynamic workflow, independently of thinking effort. Let it finish and wait for workflow results without nudging it. Much faster and more accurate than ssh + herdr. At the end: the launchers that failed their last health check, by machine (agent_start and task_plan move off them).\n\nA line under an agent is something waiting for the user: a question lists its options and the interaction id to answer it with (interaction_answer), an approval is the user's to give in the app.\n\nWrite newly generated user-facing arguments, such as titles, reasons, criteria and messages, in English unless the user requests another language or the field requires source wording. Preserve quoted source text, identifiers, tool and parameter names, enum values and command prefixes exactly. Language does not change approval requirements or permission boundaries.",
    "input_schema": {
      "type": "object",
      "properties": {
        "state": {
          "type": "string",
          "enum": [
            "active",
            "needs_you",
            "working",
            "unread",
            "idle",
            "done",
            "muted",
            "archived",
            "all"
          ],
          "description": "default active = needs_you + working + unread; done = finished work in the last 24 hours; muted = muted agents in the live snapshot categories; archived: only the archived sessions (agent_unarchive restores them); the others leave archived ones out, all lists them marked archived"
        },
        "machine": {
          "type": "string"
        },
        "query": {
          "type": "string",
          "description": "filter by title / project / directory"
        }
      }
    }
  },
  {
    "name": "agent_read",
    "description": "Read the recent conversation of one agent (from agents_list): the user's messages, what the agent said, tool calls in short form. `turns` = how many recent exchanges (default 2, max 10). Use it to answer the user's follow-ups about work an agent or task already did (its conclusion, a PR link, why it stopped) instead of asking the agent with agent_send, which makes it take another turn while the user waits (and a stopped agent takes no messages: it is read-only).\n\nWrite newly generated user-facing arguments, such as titles, reasons, criteria and messages, in English unless the user requests another language or the field requires source wording. Preserve quoted source text, identifiers, tool and parameter names, enum values and command prefixes exactly. Language does not change approval requirements or permission boundaries.",
    "input_schema": {
      "type": "object",
      "required": [
        "machine",
        "session_id"
      ],
      "properties": {
        "machine": {
          "type": "string"
        },
        "session_id": {
          "type": "string",
          "description": "full id or its first 8 characters"
        },
        "turns": {
          "type": "integer"
        }
      }
    }
  },
  {
    "name": "agent_send",
    "description": "Send what the user asked for to an Agent. A busy Agent may queue it. A stopped Agent needs resume and resume_why when its context is needed. Any stop but the user's allows resume, an unknown one too. User or exit stops need the user's Resume. It must be supervised (give task unless on an open task of yours). A nearly full Agent is compacted first. For unrelated work use agent_start. Unsure: ask_user. An unstarted branch starts on its first message. Only /exit or /quit stops a running Agent and keeps the tab. The owner's controls close another conversation's Agent.\n\nFor dialog_open, retry with dismiss_notice only when Enter alone closes the notice. For permissions and pickers, tell the user or use interaction_answer.\n\nGive task to supervise new work, including done_when in text. More on your existing task keeps its title and updates its criteria. A different title, the user's own to-do or another conversation's task is refused. A failed send creates no task. Omit task for a reply, nudge or decision.\n\nUse `handoff` for an already-stopped to-do you supervise. A node acknowledgement starts its wait for the receiving Agent's result.\n\n`wait_seconds` observes up to 50 seconds after dispatch, appending `Inline observation` JSON: `completed`(this input's `reply`), `question`(pending `interaction_id`), `timeout`/`cancelled`/`unavailable`/`interrupted`/`failed`, with known input/session ids. Observation never answers questions, accepts tasks, resends or cancels work; normal supervision/`task_review` applies. Older nodes may time out rather than return an unrelated reply.\n\nWrite newly generated user-facing arguments, such as titles, reasons, criteria and messages, in English unless the user requests another language or the field requires source wording. Preserve quoted source text, identifiers, tool and parameter names, enum values and command prefixes exactly. Language does not change approval requirements or permission boundaries.",
    "input_schema": {
      "type": "object",
      "required": [
        "machine",
        "session_id",
        "text"
      ],
      "properties": {
        "wait_seconds": {
          "type": "integer",
          "minimum": 0,
          "description": "0/omitted: normal receipt"
        },
        "machine": {
          "type": "string"
        },
        "session_id": {
          "type": "string"
        },
        "text": {
          "type": "string"
        },
        "resume": {
          "type": "boolean",
          "description": "resume this stopped agent with its context, text as its first message"
        },
        "resume_why": {
          "type": "string",
          "description": "shown to the user"
        },
        "dismiss_notice": {
          "type": "boolean",
          "description": "Press Enter first to close a notice that only says \"Enter to continue\" (no options). Any other dialog still refuses the send."
        },
        "attachments": {
          "type": "array",
          "maxItems": 8,
          "items": {
            "type": "string"
          },
          "description": "absolute paths of pictures/files the user gave in this conversation (Claude Code path or app attachment); copied to the agent, appended to its message with mentions updated; others refused"
        },
        "handoff": {
          "type": "object",
          "required": [
            "todo_id",
            "receiving_todo_id",
            "head",
            "check",
            "until"
          ],
          "additionalProperties": false,
          "properties": {
            "todo_id": {
              "type": "string"
            },
            "receiving_todo_id": {
              "type": "string"
            },
            "head": {
              "type": "string",
              "description": "Full candidate commit ID"
            },
            "check": {
              "type": "string"
            },
            "until": {
              "type": "string",
              "description": "Duration or RFC 3339 deadline"
            }
          }
        },
        "task": {
          "type": [
            "object",
            "null"
          ],
          "description": "new work: make it your supervised task first (main or a lead); null for a reply",
          "additionalProperties": false,
          "properties": {
            "title": {
              "type": "string",
              "description": "the task in a short line (English, under 20 characters)"
            },
            "done_when": {
              "type": "array",
              "maxItems": 6,
              "items": {
                "type": "string"
              },
              "description": "results you can check when it says it is done (a PR merged, a sheet's link, what the live system answers)"
            },
            "expect_minutes": {
              "type": "integer",
              "description": "how long it should take from now; longer without progress counts as stuck"
            }
          }
        }
      }
    }
  },
  {
    "name": "agent_switch",
    "description": "Move a running Claude Code agent (from agents_list) to another launcher on its computer and go on with it, in one step: the agent is quit and resumed on that launcher in the same terminal, with the same conversation, and `text` is sent to it there. It is the switch the user makes from the agent's chat. Use it only when the user asked for it, for example when a subscription's usage limit stopped agents and the user wants them to go on with another provider now rather than wait for the reset; never on your own. The launcher must be one of that computer's (machines_list and agents_list show the names). The switch happens now or not at all: an agent that is working, waits for the user (a question, a permission, a plan), is starting, has background work its quit would end, or has a draft or a dialog in its input box is refused with nothing typed and nothing left waiting; tell the user why, and try again once it has stopped if they still want it. Other refusals say why too (a launcher that does not run there or is not signed in, a model the launcher's default cannot be read for: give model). Not for stopped agents (agent_send with resume), assistant conversations, read-only agents, or Codex and other agents. An agent its usage limit stopped goes on with what it was doing when `text` is left out; any other agent needs `text`. Its usage-limit continuation, if one was due, is not sent afterwards.\n\nWrite newly generated user-facing arguments, such as titles, reasons, criteria and messages, in English unless the user requests another language or the field requires source wording. Preserve quoted source text, identifiers, tool and parameter names, enum values and command prefixes exactly. Language does not change approval requirements or permission boundaries.",
    "input_schema": {
      "type": "object",
      "required": [
        "machine",
        "session_id",
        "launcher"
      ],
      "properties": {
        "machine": {
          "type": "string",
          "description": "the machine's id or name, as agents_list shows it"
        },
        "session_id": {
          "type": "string",
          "description": "the agent's session id, or its first 8 characters"
        },
        "launcher": {
          "type": "string",
          "description": "the launcher to move it to, by its name on that computer"
        },
        "model": {
          "type": "string",
          "description": "a model of that launcher; empty: the launcher's default"
        },
        "effort": {
          "type": "string",
          "description": "the reasoning effort on the new launcher; empty: as it is"
        },
        "text": {
          "type": "string",
          "description": "what the agent does on the new launcher, sent as the user would type it; empty only for an agent its usage limit stopped (it goes on with what it was doing)"
        }
      }
    },
    "defer_loading": true
  },
  {
    "name": "agents_switch",
    "description": "Move Claude Code agents to another launcher only when the user asks. Name `agents`, or select all running and Persona-started stopped agents, filtered by `machine` and `from_launcher` (default `claude`). Preview and explain first unless the user named agents and target. Usage-limited agents switch and continue; idle agents switch without sending; working/waiting/starting/background-work agents queue until their turn ends, retaining any due limit continuation. Stopped agents change the launcher used by Resume or a resuming message. Unsupported sessions, unavailable launchers, or unreadable default models (supply `model`) are refused unchanged. Failures roll back without retry; explain why. Results are per agent: switched/continued, switched, switch-on-resume, queued, or refused. Queues expire after a day or independent launcher change; stopped agents' Resume uses the target. `agents_list` marks queues `switch_queued=`. Conversation and terminal stay unchanged.\n\nWrite newly generated user-facing arguments, such as titles, reasons, criteria and messages, in English unless the user requests another language or the field requires source wording. Preserve quoted source text, identifiers, tool and parameter names, enum values and command prefixes exactly. Language does not change approval requirements or permission boundaries.",
    "input_schema": {
      "type": "object",
      "required": [
        "launcher"
      ],
      "properties": {
        "launcher": {
          "type": "string",
          "description": "target launcher name on each agent's computer"
        },
        "model": {
          "type": "string",
          "description": "target model; empty uses the launcher default"
        },
        "effort": {
          "type": "string",
          "description": "target effort; empty preserves each agent's setting"
        },
        "agents": {
          "type": "array",
          "description": "agents to move; empty selects eligible agents after machine/from_launcher filters",
          "items": {
            "type": "object",
            "required": [
              "session_id"
            ],
            "properties": {
              "machine": {
                "type": "string",
                "description": "machine ID or name from agents_list"
              },
              "session_id": {
                "type": "string",
                "description": "session ID or its first 8 characters"
              }
            }
          }
        },
        "machine": {
          "type": "string",
          "description": "without agents, limit to this computer"
        },
        "from_launcher": {
          "type": "string",
          "description": "without agents, source launcher (default claude)"
        },
        "preview": {
          "type": "boolean",
          "description": "read-only; switches and queues nothing"
        }
      }
    },
    "defer_loading": true
  },
  {
    "name": "agent_interrupt",
    "description": "Stop what an agent (from agents_list) is doing right now, and optionally redirect it: its current turn is cut off (Esc in its terminal, or its runtime's cancel), the agent and its session stay, and `then` is sent to it at once as the user would type it. Use it when waiting is worse than stopping: it works in the wrong direction, waits in a loop (sleep, polling), repeats a failing step, collides with another agent, or the user said stop. To add a word to an agent that works fine, use agent_send instead (queued behind its turn). What it did already is not undone (files it changed, commands it ran): tell it in `then` what to put back. Not for assistant conversations, and not for agents that can only be read. A `then` of only /exit or /quit to an agent on a task another conversation supervises is refused, as agent_send refuses it: nothing is stopped or sent.\n\nWrite newly generated user-facing arguments, such as titles, reasons, criteria and messages, in English unless the user requests another language or the field requires source wording. Preserve quoted source text, identifiers, tool and parameter names, enum values and command prefixes exactly. Language does not change approval requirements or permission boundaries.",
    "input_schema": {
      "type": "object",
      "required": [
        "machine",
        "session_id"
      ],
      "properties": {
        "machine": {
          "type": "string",
          "description": "the machine's id or name, as agents_list shows it"
        },
        "session_id": {
          "type": "string",
          "description": "the agent's session id, or its first 8 characters"
        },
        "then": {
          "type": "string",
          "description": "the new instruction, sent right after it stopped; empty: just stop it"
        }
      }
    }
  },
  {
    "name": "agent_start",
    "description": "Start a new agent in the terminal engine (herdr or Orca) of one of the user's computers, in a project directory, with a first prompt. Use for real development work instead of doing it here. Check machines_list before choosing a machine. Returns the new session id. When the launcher failed its last health check on that machine, the agent starts on a machine where it works (a directory under the home moves to that home) and the answer says so. Claude-family launchers and Paseo providers with Auto Accept always start in bypassPermissions. TRAE CLI and omp take no effort setting.\n\nThe launcher decides the agent. A Claude Code launcher starts Claude Code. A launcher named pi, cursor or agy (or one the machine lists as that agent) starts that agent in a herdr pane with the model you give: pi, the Cursor CLI (cursor-agent) or the Antigravity CLI (agy). pi's first message goes through its Persona extension; cursor's and agy's go through herdr once it recognises the agent. Those three take no permission mode and no ultracode, and their effort is their own.\n\nFor parallel work needing its own checkout, give `worktree.branch`: Persona uses the machine's engine (herdr or Orca) to create and list it under the workspace containing `cwd`, then starts the agent there. Never tell the agent to run `git worktree add`: that hides it from Orca and the app. Existing branches are checked out; new ones start at that workspace's HEAD. A branch checked out elsewhere is refused.\n\nWhen the agent needs credentials the user keeps in a secret provider (a token in 1Password, say), give `secret_env`: each environment variable it should have, with the reference its value comes from (such as `op://vault/item/field`), never the secret itself. The user approves the hand-off on a card on iPhone or the web before anything starts, and the values reach only that agent's environment, for this run: you never see them, and the answer lists each variable as granted, declined, expired or failed. Only Claude Code agents on a computer this manager connects take them. Tell the agent the variables exist; never ask it to print them.\n\nGive `ultracode: true` for a large task: one that spans many files or needs checking from several angles (design, tests, review). Claude Code then runs every substantial step as a multi-agent workflow: slower and costlier: off for small work. Do not write the word \"ultracode\" into the prompt instead. Claude Code agents only (2.1.284 or later): any other agent, an older Claude Code or a node too old for it starts without it, and the answer says so. Once Claude Code confirms it, agents_list shows `ultracode=on`. An organization policy may keep some conversations read-only: a call from such a conversation is refused with the policy's reason. Do not retry; follow the refusal.\n\nWrite newly generated user-facing arguments, such as titles, reasons, criteria and messages, in English unless the user requests another language or the field requires source wording. Preserve quoted source text, identifiers, tool and parameter names, enum values and command prefixes exactly. Language does not change approval requirements or permission boundaries.\n\n`wait_seconds` observes up to 50 seconds after dispatch, appending `Inline observation` JSON: `completed`(this input's `reply`), `question`(pending `interaction_id`), `timeout`/`cancelled`/`unavailable`/`interrupted`/`failed`, with known input/session ids. Observation never answers questions, accepts tasks, resends or cancels work; normal supervision/`task_review` applies. Older nodes may time out rather than return an unrelated reply.",
    "input_schema": {
      "type": "object",
      "required": [
        "machine",
        "cwd",
        "prompt"
      ],
      "properties": {
        "wait_seconds": {
          "type": "integer",
          "minimum": 0,
          "description": "0/omitted: normal receipt"
        },
        "machine": {
          "type": "string"
        },
        "cwd": {
          "type": "string",
          "description": "absolute path or ~/…; with worktree, a directory of the repository to branch from"
        },
        "prompt": {
          "type": "string"
        },
        "skip_project_memory": {
          "type": "boolean",
          "description": "default false; true skips project/direction memory; prompt unchanged"
        },
        "attachments": {
          "type": "array",
          "maxItems": 8,
          "items": {
            "type": "string"
          },
          "description": "absolute paths of pictures/files the user gave in this conversation (Claude Code path or app attachment); copied to the agent, appended to its message with mentions updated; others refused"
        },
        "launcher": {
          "type": "string",
          "description": "listed launcher (Claude Code, pi, cursor or agy); unknown names refused with choices; empty: default/fallback, claude first, answer says which"
        },
        "secret_env": {
          "type": "object",
          "maxProperties": 8,
          "additionalProperties": {
            "type": "string"
          },
          "description": "NAME → secret-provider reference (op://vault/item/field), never a value; card approval first"
        },
        "ultracode": {
          "type": "boolean",
          "description": "multi-agent workflows; default false"
        },
        "worktree": {
          "type": "object",
          "description": "engine-made worktree of cwd's repository",
          "required": [
            "branch"
          ],
          "properties": {
            "branch": {
              "type": "string",
              "description": "the branch to check out (made from the workspace's HEAD when it does not exist)"
            }
          }
        },
        "tier": {
          "type": "string",
          "description": "Agent preset from the snapshot's 模型档位 line; choose by purpose; resolved before approval and shown on the card; explicit launcher/model/effort/speed overrides"
        },
        "speed": {
          "type": "string",
          "enum": [
            "standard",
            "fast",
            "ultrafast"
          ],
          "description": "new-start speed; explicit choice overrides the tier. standard/omitted: launcher default. Codex: fast, ultrafast; Claude Code: fast only"
        },
        "effort": {
          "type": "string",
          "description": "Explicit effort; omit for tier/purpose default."
        },
        "model": {
          "type": "string",
          "description": "Explicit model id or alias the agent takes; omit for the tier or purpose default."
        }
      }
    }
  },
  {
    "name": "agent_supervise",
    "description": "Main or a direction’s lead can adopt an Agent on no open task for supervision and task_review. Omit role for a worker. A leader needs done_when and a non-work-trust computer. It gets instructions to plan and review direct workers, who cannot delegate. On your open task, use task_scope for done_when/expect_minutes. Use agent_transfer to move another conversation's task.\n\nWrite newly generated user-facing arguments, such as titles, reasons, criteria and messages, in English unless the user requests another language or the field requires source wording. Preserve quoted source text, identifiers, tool and parameter names, enum values and command prefixes exactly. Language does not change approval requirements or permission boundaries.",
    "input_schema": {
      "type": "object",
      "required": [
        "machine",
        "session_id"
      ],
      "properties": {
        "machine": {
          "type": "string",
          "description": "agents_list machine id or name"
        },
        "session_id": {
          "type": "string",
          "description": "Agent session id or first 8 characters"
        },
        "title": {
          "type": "string",
          "description": "the task in a short line (English, under 20 characters); empty: the agent's title"
        },
        "role": {
          "type": "string",
          "enum": [
            "leader"
          ]
        },
        "done_when": {
          "type": "array",
          "maxItems": 6,
          "items": {
            "type": "string"
          },
          "description": "checkable completion criteria"
        },
        "expect_minutes": {
          "type": "integer",
          "description": "estimated minutes from now"
        }
      }
    }
  },
  {
    "name": "agent_transfer",
    "description": "Move an open Agent task to a current direction or main: main moves any; a lead hands off only its own. Keep task, brief, criteria, estimate, origin and Agent; no message, interruption or restart. Refuses user-owned to-dos, assistant sessions and untracked work (adopt with agent_supervise).\n\nWrite newly generated user-facing arguments, such as titles, reasons, criteria and messages, in English unless the user requests another language or the field requires source wording. Preserve quoted source text, identifiers, tool and parameter names, enum values and command prefixes exactly. Language does not change approval requirements or permission boundaries.",
    "input_schema": {
      "type": "object",
      "required": [
        "machine",
        "session_id",
        "supervisor"
      ],
      "properties": {
        "machine": {
          "type": "string",
          "description": "agents_list machine id or name"
        },
        "session_id": {
          "type": "string",
          "description": "Agent session id or first 8 characters"
        },
        "supervisor": {
          "type": "string",
          "description": "exact direction or main id from conversations_list"
        }
      }
    },
    "defer_loading": true
  },
  {
    "name": "agent_archive",
    "description": "Archive a coding agent's session (from agents_list) when the user asks to archive or tidy up agents: it leaves the Agents list for Archived in the app, nothing is deleted, and agent_unarchive brings it back (never remove a session any other way). Persona also asks the agent's own tool to archive it where that tool has an archive (Codex); the result says whether it did (native: done / pending / unsupported). Without close the agent itself is not closed: one open on its computer stays open. With close, its agent and its terminal tab are also closed on its computer (its transcript stays; then it is stopped until resumed): only when the user asks to close it too, and the user confirms on the phone first (declined: nothing changes). A busy agent is refused and nothing changes: working, waiting for an answer or background work, and for close also a /loop, queued messages or a server it runs. When the close does not happen the session stays archived and still open on the computer, and the result says why: tell the user exactly that, not that it was closed. With `force: true`, explicitly cancel and close an Agent stuck waiting for a terminal confirmation, then archive it, as `paseo archive --force` does. Use force only when the user asks to stop or archive that blocked Agent. Pending permissions and plans are rejected or cancelled, never allowed. Working Agents, background work, running subagents/workflows, queued messages, loops and serving ports are still refused. A failed close leaves it visible. Force implies close and does not ask for a second permission card. Not for Persona conversations (conversation_archive).\n\nWrite newly generated user-facing arguments, such as titles, reasons, criteria and messages, in English unless the user requests another language or the field requires source wording. Preserve quoted source text, identifiers, tool and parameter names, enum values and command prefixes exactly. Language does not change approval requirements or permission boundaries.",
    "input_schema": {
      "type": "object",
      "required": [
        "machine",
        "session_id"
      ],
      "properties": {
        "machine": {
          "type": "string",
          "description": "the machine's id or name, as agents_list shows it"
        },
        "session_id": {
          "type": "string",
          "description": "the agent's session id, or its first 8 characters"
        },
        "force": {
          "type": "boolean",
          "description": "cancel and close a confirmation-blocked Agent before archiving; only when the user asks; never approve its pending request"
        },
        "close": {
          "type": "boolean",
          "description": "also close its agent on the computer (asks the user on the phone first); default false: archive only"
        }
      }
    }
  },
  {
    "name": "agent_unarchive",
    "description": "Bring an archived coding agent's session back into the Agents list (find it with agents_list and state archived). Nothing is reopened on the computer: a stopped agent stays stopped until resumed (agent_send with resume). Persona also asks the agent's own tool to restore it where that tool archived it too; the result says whether it did.\n\nWrite newly generated user-facing arguments, such as titles, reasons, criteria and messages, in English unless the user requests another language or the field requires source wording. Preserve quoted source text, identifiers, tool and parameter names, enum values and command prefixes exactly. Language does not change approval requirements or permission boundaries.",
    "input_schema": {
      "type": "object",
      "required": [
        "machine",
        "session_id"
      ],
      "properties": {
        "machine": {
          "type": "string",
          "description": "the machine's id or name, as agents_list shows it"
        },
        "session_id": {
          "type": "string",
          "description": "the agent's session id, or its first 8 characters"
        }
      }
    },
    "defer_loading": true
  },
  {
    "name": "agent_autoname",
    "description": "Rename one coding agent's session with the model when the user explicitly asks for Auto Rename. This replaces the current title, including a hand-written one, with a short title generated from its conversation; it does not change the terminal tab's title. The user must confirm this write on the phone every time; a declined or unanswered confirmation does nothing and makes no model call. The background automatic-naming switch and its once-only/placeholder restrictions do not block this explicit request. Do not use it on Persona conversations (including the main chat), on an agent the user has not asked to rename, or with a session-id prefix. Use the exact machine and session_id from agents_list. On success the JSON answer has title, title_source: auto and changed; on refusal it has the stable code and retryable fields. The HTTP endpoint used by client menus is separate and does not depend on this tool.\n\nWrite newly generated user-facing arguments, such as titles, reasons, criteria and messages, in English unless the user requests another language or the field requires source wording. Preserve quoted source text, identifiers, tool and parameter names, enum values and command prefixes exactly. Language does not change approval requirements or permission boundaries.",
    "input_schema": {
      "type": "object",
      "additionalProperties": false,
      "required": [
        "machine",
        "session_id"
      ],
      "properties": {
        "machine": {
          "type": "string",
          "description": "the machine's exact id or name, as agents_list shows it"
        },
        "session_id": {
          "type": "string",
          "description": "the agent's exact full session id, never a prefix"
        }
      }
    },
    "defer_loading": true
  },
  {
    "name": "agent_autoname_receipt",
    "description": "Read-only: inspect the current assistant's existing agent_autoname confirmation cards and one coding agent's current naming snapshot. This does not prove that a particular earlier model call started, completed or succeeded: the model's tool-call id is not a manager-side correlation. The naming snapshot may predate the call or come from another caller, and background naming failures are not attributed to it. Do not retry a request from this result; unknown or ambiguous means the evidence cannot settle the outcome. Use only the exact machine and full session id from agents_list. The caller is verified from the actual assistant conversation; no other assistant's cards can be read. Optional since/before are an RFC3339 window of at most 24 hours, and must be supplied together. Confirmation history is read with bounded pages; incomplete pages are reported. This tool never calls the naming model, writes settings, approves or resolves a card, renames an agent, or publishes a session list. Not for Persona conversations, ordinary coding Agents, or extensions.\n\nWrite newly generated user-facing arguments, such as titles, reasons, criteria and messages, in English unless the user requests another language or the field requires source wording. Preserve quoted source text, identifiers, tool and parameter names, enum values and command prefixes exactly. Language does not change approval requirements or permission boundaries.",
    "input_schema": {
      "type": "object",
      "additionalProperties": false,
      "required": [
        "machine",
        "session_id"
      ],
      "properties": {
        "machine": {
          "type": "string",
          "description": "the machine's exact id, as agents_list shows it"
        },
        "session_id": {
          "type": "string",
          "description": "the agent's exact full session id, never a prefix"
        },
        "since": {
          "type": "string",
          "format": "date-time",
          "description": "inclusive RFC3339 start of the original confirmation window; requires before"
        },
        "before": {
          "type": "string",
          "format": "date-time",
          "description": "exclusive RFC3339 end of the original confirmation window [since,before); at most 24 hours after since"
        }
      }
    },
    "defer_loading": true
  },
  {
    "name": "interaction_answer",
    "description": "Answer another agent's card for the user when they said what to answer or told you to decide: each question by its own wording with an option label it offers (multi-select: an array); no free text (use agent_send). A card a 督办 message handed you is yours: answer a question or terminal dialog (an option's label or number), or allow/deny a permission, plan or dialog's Yes/No with allow, giving reason; kept as the task's decision, not the user's. Forms, secrets, approvals that send or reply in the user's name, spend money or delete, and folder-trust or Bypass Permissions dialogs are the user's: ask_escalate them. Never other approvals or the assistant's own questions.\n\nWrite newly generated user-facing arguments, such as titles, reasons, criteria and messages, in English unless the user requests another language or the field requires source wording. Preserve quoted source text, identifiers, tool and parameter names, enum values and command prefixes exactly. Language does not change approval requirements or permission boundaries.",
    "input_schema": {
      "type": "object",
      "required": [
        "machine",
        "session_id",
        "interaction_id"
      ],
      "properties": {
        "machine": {
          "type": "string",
          "description": "the machine's id or name, as agents_list shows it"
        },
        "session_id": {
          "type": "string",
          "description": "the agent's session id, or its first 8 characters"
        },
        "interaction_id": {
          "type": "string",
          "description": "the waiting interaction's id, from agents_list"
        },
        "answers": {
          "type": "object",
          "description": "each question's own wording → the option label to pick; a multi-select gives an array of labels",
          "additionalProperties": {
            "oneOf": [
              {
                "type": "string"
              },
              {
                "type": "array",
                "items": {
                  "type": "string"
                }
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        "allow": {
          "type": "boolean",
          "description": "a permission or plan card: true allows (approves), false denies"
        },
        "reason": {
          "type": "string",
          "description": "in a sentence, why this answer (kept with the task's decision)"
        }
      }
    }
  },
  {
    "name": "ask_escalate",
    "description": "Hand the cards a task's agents wait on to the user, when a 督办 message gave them to you first and only the user can decide: a form or secret; an approval that sends or replies in their name, spends money or deletes; a question that is irreversible, seen by others, changes the goal or contradicts a decision made on the task. Other permissions and plans are yours (interaction_answer); no card reaches the user on a timeout. Every card of the task the user has not been told of goes to them at once, as one reminder (with push grading on it pushes only for why_owner or p0), with your reason and recommendation; their answers go to the agents and are kept as the task's decisions. Only the task's supervisor and main can; main is told of a leader's escalation, so don't ask the user again.\n\nWrite newly generated user-facing arguments, such as titles, reasons, criteria and messages, in English unless the user requests another language or the field requires source wording. Preserve quoted source text, identifiers, tool and parameter names, enum values and command prefixes exactly. Language does not change approval requirements or permission boundaries.",
    "input_schema": {
      "type": "object",
      "required": [
        "todo_id",
        "reason"
      ],
      "properties": {
        "todo_id": {
          "type": "string",
          "description": "the task's todo id (todo_…), from the 督办 message"
        },
        "reason": {
          "type": "string",
          "description": "in a sentence, in the user's language: what they are to decide and why it is theirs"
        },
        "recommend": {
          "type": "string",
          "description": "the option you recommend, in a line (an option's label when there is one)"
        },
        "why_owner": {
          "type": "string",
          "enum": [
            "irreversible",
            "visible_to_others",
            "priority",
            "acceptance",
            "only_owner_knows"
          ],
          "description": "why only the user can decide it. In the user's push grading only irreversible, visible_to_others and only_owner_knows push to their phone; the rest wait in their Needs you list and briefing"
        },
        "p0": {
          "type": "boolean",
          "description": "true: the user must do or decide this themselves now, so it pushes even in push grading whatever why_owner says"
        }
      }
    },
    "defer_loading": true
  },
  {
    "name": "inbox_suggest",
    "description": "Submit the event’s recap and one suggestion, replacing any earlier suggestion. It waits in the user's inbox; nothing goes out until they confirm, and then the program does exactly what it says (you never send it yourself). `kind`: `reply` — one message to send, `text` in the user's voice, ready to go out as it is (no blanks to fill in like 【…】: leave out what you could not find and say so in `note`; for a Feishu sheet / Base item `changes` proposes moving rows' status: ref, field and one of the values the item lists); `agent` — an Agent to start, `text` its instruction (`machine` and `cwd` optional); `todo` — a to-do for the user to add: `text` its title (clear, starting with the action, no chat or people names), `due` only when the message states a deadline: a day `2006-01-02` for all day, or a moment as RFC 3339 with its offset; leave it out when unsure and say so in `note`; the user can edit both and adds it with one tap (a row that came to a to-do counts as handled); `conclusion` — you dealt with it, `text` the conclusion, `outcome` `fyi` (worth knowing) or `done` (nothing to do). `handback: true` for one the user has to judge: their confirmation comes back here as an instruction instead of being carried out. `note`: one or two sentences on what you found and where. `recap`: the event's background, progress and where it is stuck (up to 600 characters), every time. `title`: what the matter is, without chat or person names; `summary`: one sentence on where things stand and who is waited on for what. Write both every time; items whose title comes from the source (mail, documents, PRs / issues, and the like) ignore `title`, and the tool says so. `related`: ids of inbox items in other chats or sources this one is about. `recap` without `kind` creates no suggestion, and may include `title`, `summary`, and `follow`. `follow` records waiting on the other side: `who` (empty means waiting for a reply), `next`, and `check_at` (RFC 3339 with a time zone) or `check_in` (such as `30m`, `4h`, or `2d`). `check_at` takes precedence; with neither, look again in `24h`; the resulting time is clamped to 30 minutes–14 days from now. `follow` is valid only without `kind`. `reported`: `{text, by}` records one externally visible action you or an Agent actually completed but the program did not observe, without verifying it; never use it for plans or actions not taken. `by` is `agent` or `assistant` (default `assistant`), and `text` is one sentence, kept on one line and clipped to 120 characters. It may be given alone or accompany `kind`, `recap`, `title`, `summary`, or `follow`; a filed item refuses it, and text identical to the item's latest `reported` entry is not recorded twice. Call it at the end of each turn. In a conversation the user handed an inbox item to (its context block names the item's id), pass that id as `item` and call it when you have something to write back to the item, and again whenever it changes (it also shows in that conversation as a card); it refuses an item not handed to this conversation or one the user already filed: answer in the chat then.\n\nWrite newly generated user-facing arguments, such as titles, reasons, criteria and messages, in English unless the user requests another language or the field requires source wording. Preserve quoted source text, identifiers, tool and parameter names, enum values and command prefixes exactly. Language does not change approval requirements or permission boundaries.",
    "input_schema": {
      "type": "object",
      "properties": {
        "processing_turn": {
          "type": "string"
        },
        "item": {
          "type": "string"
        },
        "kind": {
          "type": "string",
          "enum": [
            "reply",
            "agent",
            "conclusion",
            "todo"
          ]
        },
        "text": {
          "type": "string"
        },
        "note": {
          "type": "string"
        },
        "handback": {
          "type": "boolean"
        },
        "outcome": {
          "type": "string",
          "enum": [
            "fyi",
            "done"
          ]
        },
        "recap": {
          "type": "string"
        },
        "reported": {
          "type": "object",
          "required": [
            "text"
          ],
          "properties": {
            "text": {
              "type": "string"
            },
            "by": {
              "type": "string",
              "enum": [
                "agent",
                "assistant"
              ],
              "default": "assistant"
            }
          }
        },
        "title": {
          "type": "string"
        },
        "summary": {
          "type": "string"
        },
        "follow": {
          "type": "object",
          "properties": {
            "who": {
              "type": "string"
            },
            "next": {
              "type": "string"
            },
            "check_at": {
              "type": "string",
              "format": "date-time"
            },
            "check_in": {
              "type": "string"
            }
          }
        },
        "machine": {
          "type": "string"
        },
        "cwd": {
          "type": "string"
        },
        "due": {
          "type": "string"
        },
        "related": {
          "type": "array",
          "items": {
            "type": "string"
          }
        },
        "changes": {
          "type": "array",
          "items": {
            "type": "object",
            "required": [
              "ref",
              "field",
              "value"
            ],
            "properties": {
              "ref": {
                "type": "string"
              },
              "field": {
                "type": "string"
              },
              "value": {
                "type": "string"
              }
            }
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "defer_loading": true
  },
  {
    "name": "inbox_draft",
    "description": "Legacy inbox_suggest `reply`. Prefer inbox_suggest. Draft a reply for this conversation’s inbox item. It waits in the user's inbox; they read it and send it with one tap (you never send it yourself). `text` is the reply in the user's voice, ready to go out as it is: no blanks to fill in (【…】) — leave out what you could not find and say so in `note`; `note` is one or two sentences on what you found and where, for the user to check the draft against. For a Feishu sheet / Base item, `changes` proposes moving rows' status (ref, field and one of the values the item lists); they are made when the user sends. Call it once, at the end.\n\nWrite newly generated user-facing arguments, such as titles, reasons, criteria and messages, in English unless the user requests another language or the field requires source wording. Preserve quoted source text, identifiers, tool and parameter names, enum values and command prefixes exactly. Language does not change approval requirements or permission boundaries.",
    "input_schema": {
      "type": "object",
      "required": [
        "text"
      ],
      "properties": {
        "processing_turn": {
          "type": "string"
        },
        "text": {
          "type": "string"
        },
        "note": {
          "type": "string"
        },
        "changes": {
          "type": "array",
          "items": {
            "type": "object",
            "required": [
              "ref",
              "field",
              "value"
            ],
            "properties": {
              "ref": {
                "type": "string"
              },
              "field": {
                "type": "string"
              },
              "value": {
                "type": "string"
              }
            }
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "defer_loading": true
  },
  {
    "name": "inbox_folders",
    "description": "List the folders the user's inbox items are filed under: each `/`-separated path (`work/alerts/engine`, every segment a level) with its item count and open count, and whether automatic filing by the triage model is on. Give `folder` to also list the items filed in that folder and below, or `unfiled: true` for the items in no folder; each line has the item's id (what inbox_move takes), source, title, sender and whether it is done. Call it before inbox_move, to reuse a folder in use rather than open a near-duplicate.\n\nWrite newly generated user-facing arguments, such as titles, reasons, criteria and messages, in English unless the user requests another language or the field requires source wording. Preserve quoted source text, identifiers, tool and parameter names, enum values and command prefixes exactly. Language does not change approval requirements or permission boundaries.",
    "input_schema": {
      "type": "object",
      "properties": {
        "folder": {
          "type": "string",
          "description": "a folder path: also list the items filed there and below"
        },
        "unfiled": {
          "type": "boolean",
          "description": "also list the items in no folder"
        }
      }
    },
    "defer_loading": true
  },
  {
    "name": "inbox_move",
    "description": "Move inbox items into a folder, as the user would from the inbox: `items` are their ids (from inbox_folders, or the id of an item handed to this conversation), `folder` a `/`-separated path (`work/alerts/engine`; a new path makes the folder), or `\"\"` to take them out of every folder. Moved items stay where they are put: the triage model no longer files them. An unknown id or a path with no name in it moves nothing. Move only what the user asked for.\n\nWrite newly generated user-facing arguments, such as titles, reasons, criteria and messages, in English unless the user requests another language or the field requires source wording. Preserve quoted source text, identifiers, tool and parameter names, enum values and command prefixes exactly. Language does not change approval requirements or permission boundaries.",
    "input_schema": {
      "type": "object",
      "required": [
        "items",
        "folder"
      ],
      "properties": {
        "items": {
          "type": "array",
          "items": {
            "type": "string"
          },
          "description": "inbox item ids"
        },
        "folder": {
          "type": "string",
          "description": "the folder path, or \"\" for no folder"
        }
      }
    },
    "defer_loading": true
  },
  {
    "name": "inbox_post",
    "description": "Put this conversation into the user's Inbox as a row that opens it, so a result is not missed behind a quiet unread dot. Call it only when the user explicitly asked for it (for example \"tell me if there is anything worth a look\", \"send me the briefing every time\") or when the scheduled task you are running is set to put findings in the Inbox. Never decide on your own to interrupt the user: an ordinary reply, a run with nothing new, or something merely interesting does not go there. `title` is a short name for the row (the task's name or what was found), `summary` one or two sentences saying what to look at, `tier` is `action` when the user has to do something and `fyi` (the default) when it is worth knowing, and `reason` names the request or task setting that makes this post (the user can read it on the row). Calling it again for this conversation soon after updates the same row instead of adding another. It does not push to the phone: use notify_user for that when the user asked for a push.\n\nWrite newly generated user-facing arguments, such as titles, reasons, criteria and messages, in English unless the user requests another language or the field requires source wording. Preserve quoted source text, identifiers, tool and parameter names, enum values and command prefixes exactly. Language does not change approval requirements or permission boundaries.",
    "input_schema": {
      "type": "object",
      "required": [
        "title",
        "summary",
        "reason"
      ],
      "properties": {
        "title": {
          "type": "string"
        },
        "summary": {
          "type": "string"
        },
        "tier": {
          "type": "string",
          "enum": [
            "action",
            "fyi"
          ]
        },
        "reason": {
          "type": "string"
        }
      }
    },
    "defer_loading": true
  },
  {
    "name": "kb_search",
    "description": "Search the knowledge base: summaries and metadata of chats, people, projects, docs, meetings, events, recordings (with their minutes and any transcript still retained), topics (curated knowledge points: how something works, what was decided and why, the steps and pitfalls), project timelines, the notes directories the user added (hits with an absolute path) and, with messages=true, the full message archive (newest days first). All words must match. Hits come in the order the files are read, not by relevance, so a broad search can fill the limit with chats: give kind to search one kind only, the entries whose name matches first, then as kb_list ranks them. A past meeting (its decisions, what is still open, who does what) is kind=recordings: found by its title, the calendar event's title, its minutes or any transcript still retained; then kb_get it. Transcripts expire before minutes: never promise a trimmed recording's full transcript.\n\nWrite newly generated user-facing arguments, such as titles, reasons, criteria and messages, in English unless the user requests another language or the field requires source wording. Preserve quoted source text, identifiers, tool and parameter names, enum values and command prefixes exactly. Language does not change approval requirements or permission boundaries.",
    "input_schema": {
      "type": "object",
      "required": [
        "query"
      ],
      "properties": {
        "query": {
          "type": "string"
        },
        "kind": {
          "type": "string",
          "enum": [
            "projects",
            "chats",
            "people",
            "docs",
            "meetings",
            "events",
            "recordings",
            "topics"
          ],
          "description": "only this kind's entries (no notes directories; the message archive only with chats)"
        },
        "messages": {
          "type": "boolean",
          "description": "also search the message archive (default true)"
        },
        "limit": {
          "type": "integer",
          "description": "default 40"
        }
      }
    }
  },
  {
    "name": "kb_list",
    "description": "List knowledge-base entities of one kind ranked by importance (P0 first), relevance to the user, recency: projects (short = a feature / launch, long = a team mission), chats, people, docs, meetings, events, recordings (the user's phone / watch recordings; newest first, saying which have meeting minutes, which are all-day recordings with a day recap and its state, and which transcripts have expired), topics (knowledge points curated by subject out of the timelines and chats: concepts, decisions with their reasons, practices, pitfalls, facts, open questions; the biggest first). Transcripts stay for recording_days (14 days by default), minutes and recaps for minutes_days (forever by default). Meetings, events and recordings show when they started, in the user's time zone. Optional tag filter.\n\nWrite newly generated user-facing arguments, such as titles, reasons, criteria and messages, in English unless the user requests another language or the field requires source wording. Preserve quoted source text, identifiers, tool and parameter names, enum values and command prefixes exactly. Language does not change approval requirements or permission boundaries.",
    "input_schema": {
      "type": "object",
      "required": [
        "kind"
      ],
      "properties": {
        "kind": {
          "type": "string",
          "enum": [
            "projects",
            "chats",
            "people",
            "docs",
            "meetings",
            "events",
            "recordings",
            "topics"
          ]
        },
        "tag": {
          "type": "string"
        },
        "limit": {
          "type": "integer",
          "description": "default 30"
        }
      }
    }
  },
  {
    "name": "kb_get",
    "description": "Read one entity: its metadata (links to people / chats / projects, tags), compressed context (summary.md), a project's timeline, a chat's messages of the last 7 days, a recording's meeting minutes, or an all-day recording's day recap (the day's timeline, meetings, suggested to-dos), with each action item's state (suggested, or its to-do's) and its timestamped transcript while still retained (phone/watch recordings, text only), a meeting's / event's recordings, a topic's knowledge points by subtopic (each with its kind, reason and the entries it came from). Transcripts expire after recording_days (14 days by default); minutes and recaps stay for minutes_days (forever by default). id from kb_list / kb_search (a project also by name). A project lists its 20 most relevant people and a count (all: meta.json).\n\nWrite newly generated user-facing arguments, such as titles, reasons, criteria and messages, in English unless the user requests another language or the field requires source wording. Preserve quoted source text, identifiers, tool and parameter names, enum values and command prefixes exactly. Language does not change approval requirements or permission boundaries.",
    "input_schema": {
      "type": "object",
      "required": [
        "kind",
        "id"
      ],
      "properties": {
        "kind": {
          "type": "string",
          "enum": [
            "projects",
            "chats",
            "people",
            "docs",
            "meetings",
            "events",
            "recordings",
            "topics"
          ]
        },
        "id": {
          "type": "string"
        }
      }
    }
  },
  {
    "name": "kb_update",
    "description": "Write settled facts to the KB. Projects: edit name, project_kind (short|long), status, goal, my_role, aliases, chats/people, code dirs (`\u003cmachine\u003e:\u003cpath\u003e`, linking Agents), timeline log or summary; merge_into folds duplicates, unmerge reverses it, with no other changes in that call. Annotate chats, people, docs, meetings, events, recordings (phone-owned content; tags, pinned and projects only) and topics (name, note, aliases, tags only). Explicit tags override automatic ones. remove_* (ids) permanently unlinks only that link; other updates proceed. Change project_mode/source_urls only at the user's direction: reference-only keeps pointers and aliases but leaves details at the source; full in a separate call restores detail writes.\n\nWrite newly generated user-facing arguments, such as titles, reasons, criteria and messages, in English unless the user requests another language or the field requires source wording. Preserve quoted source text, identifiers, tool and parameter names, enum values and command prefixes exactly. Language does not change approval requirements or permission boundaries.",
    "input_schema": {
      "type": "object",
      "required": [
        "kind",
        "id"
      ],
      "properties": {
