  {
    "name": "secret_run",
    "description": "Run commands on one of the user's computers that need a password only the user knows, typically its sudo password. Never ask for a password in the conversation or in ask_user: call this instead. The user gets a card in the Persona app showing the computer, your purpose and every command verbatim, types the password there and taps Run; that computer's node then runs exactly those commands in order, each as the user with /bin/sh -c in the home directory and PATH=/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin (give other tools by their absolute path). Only a command that runs sudo with -S gets the password, as a line on its stdin; every other command gets an empty stdin. Write sudo as `sudo -S -k -p '' …`, one sudo per command and never after a pipe (sudo would read the pipe; run a pipeline as root with `sudo -S -k -p '' sh -c '…'`); a sudo without -S is refused. Where a sudoers rule says NOPASSWD, sudo does not read stdin and the command it runs gets the password instead, so do not give such a command anything that reads stdin. Commands are printable ASCII only, so the card shows exactly what runs. The first command that fails stops the run. Never write a command that prints, stores or sends the password; you never see it. The result is JSON with status (ran, failed, declined, expired or refused), the computer, a message and, for ran and failed, each command's exit_code, timed_out and output (stdout and stderr, clipped to its end); when a command's output held the password, none of it comes back (output_withheld: true). A command stopped at the timeout may still run as root: check before asking again. declined, expired and refused mean nothing ran: tell the user; ask again only if they want. Not on Windows.\n\nWrite newly generated user-facing arguments, such as titles, reasons, criteria and messages, in English unless the user requests another language or the field requires source wording. Preserve quoted source text, identifiers, tool and parameter names, enum values and command prefixes exactly. Language does not change approval requirements or permission boundaries.",
    "input_schema": {
      "type": "object",
      "required": [
        "machine",
        "purpose",
        "commands"
      ],
      "properties": {
        "machine": {
          "type": "string",
          "description": "the computer to run on: its id or name, as agent_start takes it"
        },
        "purpose": {
          "type": "string",
          "description": "one sentence the card shows: why the password is needed"
        },
        "commands": {
          "type": "array",
          "items": {
            "type": "string"
          },
          "minItems": 1,
          "maxItems": 5,
          "description": "1 to 5 one-line shell commands of printable ASCII, at most 1000 characters each, run in order"
        },
        "delivery": {
          "type": "string",
          "enum": [
            "stdin"
          ],
          "description": "how the password reaches the commands: stdin, the default and only way"
        },
        "timeout_seconds": {
          "type": "integer",
          "minimum": 1,
          "maximum": 1800,
          "description": "for the whole run, default 300"
        }
      },
      "additionalProperties": false
    },
    "defer_loading": true
  },
  {
    "name": "skill_import",
    "description": "Import a user-requested skill ZIP through Persona's existing Settings importer. First preview the base64-encoded archive (up to 4 MiB compressed); returns validated metadata, a short-lived reference and environment. Then install with the same archive, reference and environment. Installation and undo wait for the user's own permission card showing the validated skill and target computer; never approve for the user. The target is the current assistant's configured personal computer, never a model-chosen path or machine. Skills are shared by assistants using that computer account and load in new conversations; an existing conversation is not guaranteed to refresh. Same-name skills are never overwritten. ZIP inspection does not run scripts or treat skill instructions as authorization. Use status with the original reference and environment after a lost/uncertain response, before any retry: it records previewed, installed or undone and recovers the undo receipt. Status is an operation receipt, not a live inventory; receipts expire after ten minutes or a computer restart. If unavailable, inspect Settings \u003e Skills instead of assuming failure. Cancel discards an unused preview. Undo takes an installed receipt, refuses edited/replaced files and retains recovery data. No URL download, repository clone, arbitrary path write or arbitrary skill removal is provided. An older computer may require a Persona update.\n\nWrite newly generated user-facing arguments, such as titles, reasons, criteria and messages, in English unless the user requests another language or the field requires source wording. Preserve quoted source text, identifiers, tool and parameter names, enum values and command prefixes exactly. Language does not change approval requirements or permission boundaries.",
